Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear TMB Experts,

I have developed a delta model to standardize CPUE. The model runs well using a conventional delta formulation as follows:

Type prob = 1 / (1 + exp(-eta1)); // Encounter probability

Type pcat= exp(eta2); // Positive catch in number

Type mu = prob* pcat;

However, when I switched to a Poisson-link model, I encountered the following error:

Error in iterate(par): Newton dropout because inner gradient had non-finite components.

Warning message:

In nlminb(obj$par, obj$fn, obj$gr) : NA/NaN function evaluation.

In my Poisson-link formulation, I used:

// Poisson-link

Type lambda = exp(eta1);

Type log_prob = logspace_sub(Type(0.0), -lambda);

Type prob = exp(log_prob);

 // Positive

Type log_pcat = eta1 + eta2  - log_prob;

// Prob * positive

Type mu = exp(eta1 + eta2);

I suspect that the error might be due to numerical issues arising from the use of logspace_sub and the subsequent exponentiation, which might be causing the gradient to produce non-finite values.

Could you please advise on possible modifications or improvements to stabilize the numerical calculations in this Poisson-link model? Any guidance or suggestions would be greatly appreciated.

Thanks!

